# mem_singleton_root.tkr — the NATIVE-route regressor of `#singleton` binding residence (0.3.1
# modelo-de-memoria-por-escopo §2a / crumb C2b).
#
# WHAT IT PINS. A local binding may now carry `#singleton` (the declarative root-residence surface,
# opened for bindings by crumb C2b): the parser accepts `#singleton let|mut|const …`, threads the
# lifetime onto the binding, and the residence oracle (`src/checker/residence.tks`) forces such a
# binding to `ResidenceTier::Root`. `#scoped`/`#transient` on a binding stay rejected.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. `probe` declares a `#singleton` str binding, then runs an inner loop that opens and
# closes allocating scopes (the churn) BEFORE reading the singleton back. A `#singleton` binding
# resides in root, so it survives the churn and reads back its exact value — it did NOT die with any
# `{}`. (The oracle's residence routing is additive foundation; nobody consumes the plan yet, so the
# emitted program is unchanged and the value survives on today's leak-everything backend too. This
# fixture is the STANDING regression that keeps proving it once the C3/C4 activation lands.)
#
# NATIVE by construction: no env is declared, so `When built and run` uses the default (native)
# backend, exactly as `one_byte_native.tkr` does. The C route is covered by the same source through
# the own-vs-C differential.

Feature: mem_singleton_root — a `#singleton` binding resides in root and survives scope churn

  Scenario: a #singleton str binding read after inner allocating scopes keeps its exact value
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "root-anchor"
